            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for to rock, then the German for the captain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der Anker",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Anker — \"the anchor\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Der Anker fällt ins Wasser. The anchor drops into the water."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Same form in the plural. Vor Anker liegen — to lie at anchor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Anker is an early borrowing from Latin ancora 'anchor' (Lewis & Short), already in Old High German as anker (EtymDB). English anchor comes from the same Latin word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
